    <mj-text
      padding="10px 12px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        UAE law reset
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-image
          border-radius="10px"
          padding="10px 12px"
          width="600px"
          src="https://www.dubaisummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
          alt="REPLACE_ME"
          href="https://www.dubaisummary.com/"
          target="_blank"
        />

    <mj-text padding="10px 12px 16px 12px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s happening? </strong>The UAE will introduce a <a href="https://gulfnews.com/living-in-uae/ask-us/major-uae-law-changes-from-june-1-legal-adulthood-lowered-from-21-to-18-1.500405656" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #102341; color: black;">Civil Transactions Law</a> from June 1, updating rules on judicial capacity, contracts, civil dealings, guardianship profiles, asset management, and compensation. The biggest change lowers the age of judicial adulthood from 21 to 18, giving 18-year-olds full judicial capacity to manage civil and monetary affairs. The law may also allow minors as young as 15 to manage assets with court approval.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Other updates:</strong> Possible contract registration rules, making some agreements valid or enforceable after official registration. The law may also clarify what happens to expat assets in the UAE when someone dies without a will or heirs. It may introduce stronger duties during pre-contract negotiations, especially around disclosure. Buyer protections could also be expanded, allowing people to reject defective goods, seek a reduction, or appeal for replacements.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Big picture:</strong> The UAE Civil Transactions Law reflects a wider push to modernise personal, commercial, and monetary rules. The law appears to strengthen safeguards through court oversight, clearer contract rules, stronger buyer protections, and more detailed procedures for inheritance gaps. For residents, businesses, and expats, the changes could make civil dealings more structured and predictable, while also increasing the need to understand registration, disclosure, and judicial responsibility before entering into agreements.</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
